  "grammar": [
    { "id": "adjectives", "title": "Adjectives of every type", "concept": "gender", "body": [
      "Type 1 (bonus, bona, bonum) you know. Type 2 is third-declension: **fortis** (m./f.), **forte** (n.); gen. **fortis**; abl. **fortī**; plural **fortēs, fortia**.",
      "Adjectives agree in gender, number and case, not in ending shape: **vir fortis**, **fēmina fortis**, **corpus forte**, **morbus gravis**."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Medicus fortis aegrōs gravēs cūrat.", "en": "The brave doctor treats seriously ill patients." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "adj1", "prompt": "Say: a strong man", "answer": "vir fortis" },
      { "id": "adj2", "prompt": "Say: a brave woman", "answer": "fēmina fortis" },
      { "id": "adj3", "prompt": "Say: a serious disease", "answer": "morbus gravis" },
      { "id": "adj4", "prompt": "Say: healthy bodies", "answer": "corpora sāna" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "medicine", "title": "Latin in medicine", "body": [
      "Anatomy is still named in Latin (Terminologia Anatomica): **os femoris** (bone of the thigh), **vena cava** (hollow vein), **musculus biceps brachiī** (two-headed muscle of the arm), **corpus callōsum**.",
      "Clinical English is full of Latin: **ocular, oral, manual, pedal, cordial, sanguine, febrile, morbid**. Greek supplies the rest (cardio-, gastro-, -itis); that comes with the Greek pack.",
      "Health talk uses the dative: **caput mihi dolet** = my head hurts (lit. the head hurts to me)."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Ubi tibi dolet? Pēs mihi dolet.", "en": "Where does it hurt? My foot hurts." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "med1", "prompt": "Say: My head hurts.", "answer": "Caput mihi dolet." },
      { "id": "med2", "prompt": "Ask: What hurts (you)?", "answer": "Quid tibi dolet?" },
      { "id": "med3", "prompt": "Say: I have a fever.", "answer": "Febrem habeō." },
      { "id": "med4", "prompt": "Say: The doctor treats the sick man.", "answer": "Medicus aegrum cūrat." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "perfect", "title": "Perfect tense: vēnī, vīdī, vīcī", "concept": "aspect", "body": [
      "The perfect is a completed past: **-ī, -istī, -it, -imus, -istis, -ērunt** on the perfect stem: amāv-ī, vīd-ī, vēn-ī, dīx-ī, fēc-ī, scrīps-ī, fu-ī (I was).",
      "Learn the perfect with the verb (the dictionary's third form). Spanish preterite comes from it: amāvī → amé, vīdī → vi, fēcī → hice, dīxī → dije."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Herī Mārcus epistulam scrīpsit et ad urbem iit.", "en": "Yesterday Marcus wrote a letter and went to the city." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "pf1", "prompt": "Say: I came, I saw, I conquered.", "answer": "Vēnī, vīdī, vīcī." },
      { "id": "pf2", "prompt": "Ask: What did you do?", "answer": "Quid fēcistī?" },
      { "id": "pf3", "prompt": "Say: Marcus wrote a letter.", "answer": "Mārcus epistulam scrīpsit." },
      { "id": "pf4", "prompt": "Say: They came.", "answer": "Vēnērunt." },
      { "id": "pf5", "prompt": "Say: Yesterday I was at school.", "answer": "Herī in scholā fuī." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "imperfect", "title": "Imperfect: was …-ing, used to", "concept": "aspect", "body": [
      "The imperfect describes ongoing or repeated past: **-bam, -bās, -bat, -bāmus, -bātis, -bant**: amābam, vidēbam, dīcēbam, audiēbam. 'To be': **eram, erās, erat, erāmus, erātis, erant**.",
      "Spanish -aba/-ía is the same tense: amābat → amaba. Stories set the scene in the imperfect and move the action in the perfect: **Lupus bibēbat; subitō agnus vēnit**."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Ōlim rēx in urbe habitābat; cīvēs eum amābant.", "en": "Once a king lived in the city; the citizens loved him." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "ipf1", "prompt": "Say: He used to live in Rome.", "answer": "Rōmae habitābat." },
      { "id": "ipf2", "prompt": "Say: There was a king.", "answer": "Rēx erat." },
      { "id": "ipf3", "prompt": "Say: We were laughing.", "answer": "Rīdēbāmus." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "demonstratives", "title": "this, that, he: hic, ille, is", "body": [
      "**hic, haec, hoc** = this (near me) · **ille, illa, illud** = that (over there) · **is, ea, id** = he, she, it / that one (just mentioned).",
      "Spanish turned **ille, illa** into the articles **el, la** and the pronoun **él**; **iste** became **este**."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Hic puer Mārcus est; ille vir pater eius est.", "en": "This boy is Marcus; that man is his father." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "dm1", "prompt": "Say: this book", "answer": "hic liber" },
      { "id": "dm2", "prompt": "Say: that girl", "answer": "illa puella" },
      { "id": "dm3", "prompt": "Say: I see him.", "answer": "Eum videō." },
      { "id": "dm4", "prompt": "Say: this gift", "answer": "hoc dōnum" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "relative", "title": "who, which: quī, quae, quod", "body": [
      "The relative pronoun takes its **gender and number** from the noun it refers to and its **case** from its own clause: **puella quae cantat** (subject → nom.), **liber quem legō** (object → acc.), **puer cuius pater medicus est** (whose → gen.).",
      "Spanish 'que' and 'quien' come from it."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Agnus quem lupus videt parvus est.", "en": "The lamb which the wolf sees is small." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "rl1", "prompt": "Say: The girl who is singing is my sister.", "answer": "Puella quae cantat soror mea est." },
      { "id": "rl2", "prompt": "Say: The book which I am reading is new.", "answer": "Liber quem legō novus est." },
      { "id": "rl3", "prompt": "Say: the boy whose father is a doctor", "answer": "puer cuius pater medicus est" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "participle", "title": "Participles: dīvīsa, facta, scrīpta", "concept": "participle", "body": [
      "The perfect passive participle (4th dictionary form) is an adjective meaning 'having been …-ed': **dīvīsus, -a, -um** = divided; **factus** = made; **scrīptus** = written; **amātus** = loved. Spanish: dividido, hecho, escrito, amado.",
      "With **est/sunt** it makes the passive perfect: **epistula scrīpta est** = the letter was written; **facta est lūx** = light was made.",
      "Greek has even more participles; the Greek pack links back here."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Gallia in partēs trēs dīvīsa est.", "en": "Gaul has been divided into three parts." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "pt1", "prompt": "Say: divided (feminine)", "answer": "dīvīsa" },
      { "id": "pt2", "prompt": "Say: The letter was written.", "answer": "Epistula scrīpta est." },
      { "id": "pt3", "prompt": "Say: Light was made.", "answer": "Facta est lūx." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "reading", "title": "How to read real Latin", "body": [
      "1 · Real texts have **no macrons**. Turn macrons off in Settings now and then to practise.",
      "2 · Find the **verb** (often at the end), then its **subject** (nominative), then the **object** (accusative). Everything else hangs on these.",
      "3 · **-que** means 'and' joined to the second word: **Dīxitque** = and he said. **est** is often left out: **Gallia dīvīsa** = Gaul (is) divided.",
      "4 · Read each sentence twice: once for gist, once for structure. Don't translate word by word into English; picture it."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Dīxitque Deus: fīat lūx.", "en": "And God said: let there be light." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "rd1", "prompt": "Latin for 'and he said' (with -que)", "answer": "dīxitque" },
      { "id": "rd2", "prompt": "Say: Gaul (is) divided into three parts.", "answer": "Gallia dīvīsa in partēs trēs." }
    ], "verified": false }
  ],
  "texts": [
    { "id": "d04", "title": "Apud medicum", "level": "U9", "kind": "dialogue", "names": ["Venu"], "lines": [
      { "sp": "Medicus", "text": "Salvē! Quid tibi dolet?", "en": "Hello! What hurts?" },
      { "sp": "Venu", "text": "Caput mihi dolet, et febrem habeō.", "en": "My head hurts, and I have a fever." },
      { "sp": "Medicus", "text": "Ex quō tempore?", "en": "Since when?" },
      { "sp": "Venu", "text": "Ab herī.", "en": "Since yesterday." },
      { "sp": "Medicus", "text": "Ōs aperī, quaesō. Bene. Cor sānum est.", "en": "Open your mouth, please. Good. Your heart is healthy." },
      { "sp": "Venu", "text": "Estne morbus gravis?", "en": "Is the illness serious?" },
      { "sp": "Medicus", "text": "Minimē! Nōn gravis est. Dormī multum et aquam bibe.", "en": "Not at all! It's not serious. Sleep a lot and drink water." },
      { "sp": "Venu", "text": "Grātiās tibi agō, medice!", "en": "Thank you, doctor!" },
      { "sp": "Medicus", "text": "Valē, et cūrā tē!", "en": "Goodbye, and take care of yourself!" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "r06", "title": "Herī", "level": "U10", "kind": "reading", "names": ["Venu", "Caesar", "Caesare", "Asiam", "Rōmam"], "lines": [
      { "text": "Herī diēs longus erat.", "en": "Yesterday was a long day." },
      { "text": "Prīmum Venu in scholā fuit; magister fābulam dē Caesare nārrāvit.", "en": "First Venu was at school; the teacher told a story about Caesar." },
      { "text": "Caesar dux Rōmānus erat. Ōlim ad Asiam vēnit et hostēs celeriter vīcit.", "en": "Caesar was a Roman general. Once he came to Asia and quickly defeated the enemy." },
      { "text": "Tum Caesar Rōmam epistulam mīsit: “Vēnī, vīdī, vīcī.”", "en": "Then Caesar sent a letter to Rome: “I came, I saw, I conquered.”" },
      { "text": "Postquam Venu domum vēnit, aquam bibit et librum lēgit.", "en": "After Venu came home, he drank water and read a book." },
      { "text": "Deinde epistulam ad mātrem scrīpsit. Tandem nocte dormīvit.", "en": "Then he wrote a letter to his mother. At last, at night, he slept." },
      { "text": "Crās iterum in scholā erit!", "en": "Tomorrow he'll be at school again!" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "r07", "title": "Lupus et agnus (after Phaedrus)", "level": "U11", "kind": "reading", "source": "Simplified retelling of Phaedrus, Fābulae 1.1 (public domain), by Claude", "lines": [
      { "text": "Ōlim lupus et agnus ad rīvum vēnērunt, quod sitim habēbant.", "en": "Once a wolf and a lamb came to a stream, because they were thirsty." },
      { "text": "Aqua ā lupō ad agnum fluēbat.", "en": "The water flowed from the wolf to the lamb." },
      { "text": "Lupus, quī agnum capere cupiēbat, clāmāvit: “Cūr aquam meam malam facis?”", "en": "The wolf, who wanted to catch the lamb, shouted: “Why are you making my water bad?”" },
      { "text": "Agnus timēbat, sed respondit: “Quōmodo hoc faciō? Aqua ā tē ad mē fluit!”", "en": "The lamb was afraid, but answered: “How am I doing that? The water flows from you to me!”" },
      { "text": "Lupus iterum dīxit: “Ante sex mēnsēs dē mē mala dīxistī!”", "en": "The wolf spoke again: “Six months ago you said bad things about me!”" },
      { "text": "Agnus respondit: “Tum nōndum nātus eram.”", "en": "The lamb answered: “Then I had not yet been born.”" },
      { "text": "“Pater ergō tuus dē mē mala dīxit!” clāmāvit lupus, et agnum cēpit.", "en": "“Then your father said bad things about me!” shouted the wolf, and he seized the lamb." },
      { "text": "Haec fābula dē hominibus est quī sine causā aliōs opprimunt.", "en": "This fable is about people who oppress others without cause." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "a01", "title": "Caesar, Dē Bellō Gallicō 1.1", "level": "U12", "kind": "authentic", "names": ["Belgae", "Aquitani", "Celtae", "Galli"], "source": "C. Iulius Caesar, Commentarii de Bello Gallico 1.1, opening (public domain). Shown without macrons, as in real editions.", "lines": [
      { "text": "Gallia est omnis divisa in partes tres, quarum unam incolunt Belgae, aliam Aquitani, tertiam qui ipsorum lingua Celtae, nostra Galli appellantur.", "en": "Gaul as a whole is divided into three parts, of which the Belgae inhabit one, the Aquitani another, and the third those who in their own language are called Celts, in ours Gauls." },
      { "text": "Hi omnes lingua, institutis, legibus inter se differunt.", "en": "All these differ from one another in language, customs and laws." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "a02", "title": "Vulgate, Genesis 1:1–5", "level": "U12", "kind": "authentic", "source": "Biblia Sacra Vulgata, Genesis 1:1–5 (public domain). Shown without macrons.", "lines": [
      { "text": "In principio creavit Deus caelum et terram.", "en": "In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth." },
      { "text": "Terra autem erat inanis et vacua, et tenebrae erant super faciem abyssi, et spiritus Dei ferebatur super aquas.", "en": "The earth was without form and empty, and darkness was upon the face of the deep, and the spirit of God moved over the waters." },
      { "text": "Dixitque Deus: Fiat lux. Et facta est lux.", "en": "And God said: Let there be light. And there was light." },
      { "text": "Et vidit Deus lucem quod esset bona, et divisit lucem a tenebris.", "en": "And God saw the light, that it was good, and he divided the light from the darkness." },
      { "text": "Appellavitque lucem Diem, et tenebras Noctem. Factumque est vespere et mane, dies unus.", "en": "And he called the light Day, and the darkness Night. And there was evening and morning, one day." }
    ], "verified": false }
  ]
}
